<title>USA: Ohio corn, soy harvest slowed considerably by rain.</title>
<headline>Ohio corn, soy harvest slowed considerably by rain.</headline>
<dateline>COLUMBUS, Ohio 1996-10-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>Rain and the resulting muddy fields slowed harvest activity in Ohio considerably during the week ended Sunday, according to the state's Agricultural Statistics Service.</p>
<p>Soybeans were 59 percent harvested, up from 45 percent a week earlier but still two weeks behind the five-year average of 89 percent.</p>
<p>Nearly all the soybean crop was mature, the report said.</p>
<p>Corn was 22 percent harvested, up from 15 percent a week earlier but more than two weeks behind the average pace of 50 percent.</p>
<p>Farmers have completed 82 percent of the intended winter wheat seeding, but progress remained more than a week behind the five-year average of 91 percent.</p>
<p>Wheat was 53 percent emerged versus 70 percent during the same period a year ago and the five-year average of 63 percent.</p>
<p>--Chicago newsdesk 312-408 8720</p>
</text>
